{
 "title": "Unix System Programming in OCaml",
 "byline": "Xavier Leroy and Didier Rémy · course notes, English edition",
 "note": "Answers to the exercises (PDF pp. 125–138) are not broken down. Each chapter and section also links to the free HTML edition on ocaml.github.io. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "A course on Unix system programming that uses OCaml’s Unix library, so each system call is seen through a typed functional interface while the concepts stay the classic ones. It covers files and the file system, processes and their lifecycle, signals, pipes and redirection, sockets for network clients and servers, and threads with locks, conditions and events. Most chapters are built around complete working programs: find, cp, tar, a mini-shell, a parallel sieve, a universal client and server, an HTTP relay. Along the way it is candid about the hard parts: buffering costs, race conditions with signals, and the difficulties of combining threads with Unix calls.",
